  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\n\nRainy conditions should carry over into Sunday with areawide PoPs of \n60 to 80% forecast as the H8 baroclinic zone swings northeastward. \n\nBy Monday morning, should see an intensifying shortwave trof eject \nfrom the Montana/Alberta area east northeast toward the \nDakotas/Manitoba. The feature will spawn another sfc low that tracks \nnorth of the area, while the sfc front swings through most of the \nNorthern Plains. Ahead of the frontal feature, expect gusty 30-35 \nmph southwesterly winds across the area. A plume of 500-1,000 J/kg \nof instability will also overspread the area, and combined with sfc \nconvergence along the front, could see more convection develop. \nStrong to severe convection could develop, but it's worth noting \nthat the bulk of 0-6 km shear is located near to just behind the \nfrontal boundary and not collocated in the best plume of \ninstability. Better H7-H5 midlevel forcing appears once again \nfocused in the eastern Dakotas and Minnesota area. Some guidance\ndoes hint at a subtle wave trekking through the Central Plains \nduring the day which could help provide more large scale lift. \nAt this time, PoPs of 60 to 80% are forecast for most of \nsoutheast Nebraska into western Iowa Monday evening into \nTuesday.\n\nPoP chances continue into Wednesday and Thursday as we remain \nin a zonal to slightly southwesterly flow and continued \ndisturbances ride it. Temperatures in the long term will range \nfrom the 70s to low 80s Sunday, followed by a brief warm up \nMonday to the 80s and low 90s, then cooling down to the 60s to \nlow 70s (yay, fall!) Tuesday through Thursday.",
